Let $\ell \in \mathbb{N}_0$. We will show first that the set $L(\ell)$ is nonempty. It follows from \eqref{eqn_stronger_than_cssd} that there exists $n \in 2^\ell \mathbb{N}$ such that $n +  ig(A \cap [2^\ell] ig) \subseteq A$. Let $m \in [n]$ be the greatest integer such that $n \in W(m)$. If $m=0$, then by \cref{lemma_punches_at_new_elements}, we have that $|W(n)| \geqslant 2^\ell$, whereby $n \in L(\ell)$. Otherwise, we have that $1 \leqslant m < n$ and $n \in W(m)$, whereby $ ig( W(m)  ackslash \{m\}  ig) \cap 2^\ell \mathbb{N} 
eq \emptyset$. It follows from \cref{lemma_basic_dyadic_cubes_facts} that $|W(m)| \geqslant 2^{\ell + 1}$, so $m \in L(\ell)$. By \eqref{eqn_stronger_than_cssd}, \cref{lemma_syndetic_characterization}, and the previous paragraph, there exists $k \in \mathbb{N}$ so that  egin{align} 2^\ell \mathbb{N}_0 \cap  igcap_{a \in A \cap [2^{\ell}]} (A-a) \in \mathcal{Q}_{2^{k}}^* \end{align} and so that $L(\ell) \cap [2^k] 
eq \emptyset$. We will show that $L(\ell)$ is syndetic by showing that $L(\ell) \in \mathcal{Q}_{2^{k+1}}^*$ and appealing again to \cref{lemma_syndetic_characterization}. To show that $L(\ell) \in \mathcal{Q}_{2^{k+1}}^*$, we will show that for all $a \in 2^{k+1}\mathbb{N}_0$, the set $L(\ell) \cap  ig(a + ([2^{k+1}] ackslash \{0\}) ig)$ is nonempty. We proceed by induction on $a$. We have already shown the base case $a=0$. (Recall that, by definition, $L(\ell) \subseteq \mathbb{N}$, so that $L(\ell) \cap [2^k] 
eq \emptyset$ implies $L(\ell) \cap ([2^k]  ackslash \{0\}) 
eq \emptyset$.) Let $a \in 2^{k+1}\mathbb{N}$, and define $Q = a + [2^{k+1}]$. From \eqref{eqn_cube_syndeticity_from_upgraded_membership_property}, there exists \[n \in  ig( Q  ackslash \{a\}  ig) \cap 2^\ell \mathbb{N}_0 \cap  igcap_{a \in A \cap [2^{\ell}]} (A-a).\] Let $m \in [n]$ be the greatest integer such that $n \in W(m)$. If $m=0$, then by \cref{lemma_punches_at_new_elements}, we have that $|W(n)| \geqslant 2^\ell$, whereby $n \in L(\ell) \cap  ig( Q  ackslash \{a\}  ig)$, as desired. Otherwise, we have that $1 \leqslant m < n$. If $m > a$, then $m \in Q  ackslash \{a\}$. Since $m < n$ and $n \in W(m)$, we have that $ ig( W(m)  ackslash \{m\}  ig) \cap 2^\ell \mathbb{N} 
eq \emptyset$. It follows from \cref{lemma_basic_dyadic_cubes_facts} that $|W(m)| \geqslant 2^{\ell + 1}$, so $m \in L(\ell) \cap  ig( Q  ackslash \{a\}  ig)$, as desired. If, on the other hand, $m \leqslant a$, then the fact that $W(m)$ and $Q$ are intersecting dyadic intervals (both contain $n$) implies that $m \in 2^{k+1} \mathbb{N}$, that $Q \subseteq W(m)$, and that $Q - m \in \mathcal{Q}_{2^{k+1}}$. It follows by the induction hypothesis that there exists $q \in Q  ackslash \{a\}$ such that $q-m \in L(\ell) \cap (Q-m)$. Thus, $|W(q-m)| \geqslant 2^\ell$. Since $q \in W(m)$ and $m \leqslant a < q$, it follows from \cref{lemma_fractal_windows} that $W(q-m) = W(q) - m$. Therefore, $|W(q)| \geqslant 2^\ell$, whereby $q \in L(\ell) \cap (Q  ackslash \{a\})$, as desired.

